From 229844da3510ef31602d6133e06ff4fcbc66669c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joecorrell <joecorrell03@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 20:14:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] feat(video): capture and encode a second display into the
 second queue

The half that was missing: something to produce the picture. A second display
now gets its own capture thread, pinned to its own output, feeding
mail::video_packets2 where stream.cpp's second sender is already waiting.

sync_session_ctx_t gains an output_name_override, and encode_run_sync honours
it by resolving the display once and ignoring both the configured output and
the display-switch hotkey. Both of those describe the screen the game is on; a
second-display session that followed either would chase the game onto its
display and send the same picture down both streams.

capture_second_display always takes the synchronous path, never capture_async.
The asynchronous capture thread holds one display and shares it between every
session queued on it -- correct for several clients watching one screen, and
exactly wrong here, where a different screen is the entire point. Routing
through it would have silently streamed the game's display twice. The cost is
the parallel encoder's throughput on a stream that is usually a static desktop.

videoThread2 carries no fail guard, unlike videoThread. Losing the desktop
screen must cost a screen rather than the game being played -- the host-side
half of the rule SecondStreamListenerCallbacks follows on the client. It also
waits for the client to ping the second video port before capturing, so a
client that negotiates and never connects costs a timeout rather than an
encoder session producing frames with nowhere to go.

The lease is held on the session, so a virtual monitor acquired for a client
disappears when that client does rather than outliving it on the desktop.
---
 src/stream.cpp |  87 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 src/video.cpp  | 135 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 src/video.h    |  21 ++++++++
 3 files changed, 239 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

